2}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398578} :answer-field/id #uuid "4112b8f8-a1d3-5ca1-a272-50f00bc81e5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b2987e81-f85b-5262-9ea2-835ea44d86e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162644" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(t+1)\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t)\\,j+{e}^{-t}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $t+1$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t)$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\n3t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component ${e}^{-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."} :step/id #uuid "3ce5a87f-f4ad-59bf-8bf7-3a76cd4d166e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d759664-71cf-5524-8292-b4f9f56698b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in (-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279292} {:answer/id #uuid "839d4983-b3e1-5df2-929b-e864ccf4fcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in (-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279769} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed76513-0186-5831-b557-a4f08af7383f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in (-1,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279863} {:answer/id #uuid "ad393dc1-dc55-547e-b037-d30e59fc192b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280126} {:answer/id #uuid "b43b6b14-c660-5371-ab23-f23367846209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280126} :answer-field/id #uuid "685912e0-327e-5f2f-962f-61ee3eb96008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0b92442b-8b2c-57b7-8241-452f363996f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162662" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{t}$ is $t\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ is $s\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\frac{1}{t}$ is  $t\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/97/971a65619088884002784f5abb3c4f3dc9b2e17e6b905f171b8106219a139557.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (0,\\infty ).$"} :step/id #uuid "3f69fac6-c791-5076-b0cb-1df861e3f01d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b740762-045e-5820-a736-1fe5651b59de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (-2,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363460} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f05f0a-6614-5d5d-9df5-da7e78375081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (-2,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363461} {:answer/id #uuid "43be1e1f-d373-53b1-afef-1ef4c39e04b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363462} {:answer/id #uuid "aef37efd-8619-5880-9e33-8cc7399e16db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363463} {:answer/id #uuid "3bcc0512-6302-5ee0-b419-66a663e9083e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363463} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e9a1fab-e156-581f-bef9-19ac8957daa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7f5ab3ef-ae4b-58ea-a096-4f661af5c41f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162663" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)=\\sqrt{1-s}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+2)\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-s)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f,$ we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{1-s}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,1].$\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+2)$ is $s\\in (-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-s)$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/6a/6a2d170c22a544335735e859dd080d74964a4d5b5053789a557b5ea406f9d3a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is $s\\in (-2,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "649a877d-0747-58ab-aefc-0fc49ca6326f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5826ca19-6daa-5fae-a51f-ca9bd88aa67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 1759