 :step/id #uuid "70d11093-23ab-55b7-88e4-81ad27b59786" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4e7ea98-c99e-5e1a-b0a7-704c7552fe2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363420} {:answer/id #uuid "516f0376-d461-50f1-9446-88d42e4dd246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363421} {:answer/id #uuid "606a6900-ad5d-54ed-92c9-7c9c48c4ff11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363422} {:answer/id #uuid "df8725b2-1bdf-5492-bb7d-22cf79b754fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363423} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b51453-7d1a-5d7d-92fc-c5d50c4ca073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363424}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363424} :answer-field/id #uuid "669d7279-38d4-5150-94a0-6a97a679c0f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b7cf4d71-17d1-5d39-bf9c-739bde65f8a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160767" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."} :step/id #uuid "afc5168b-5251-58d8-8fc1-8965074ea6cd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411591}}] :activity/title "The Domain of a Vector-Valued Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411590}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420096} :activity/id #uuid "d63840d2-3609-5c32-bdc9-4fe9ce0a42cd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049186 :topic/id #uuid "e1c10e80-3811-5fcd-98a3-8118c49347e4" :topic/math-academy-id 1737} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54811e57-5329-5901-9031-55945bdb3aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420082} {:answer/id #uuid "656b42d3-126c-5abb-9880-9e0fc6a9e621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420083} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5d9399-1eaf-57c4-b24e-dd1b89bcd1e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi n}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420085} {:answer/id #uuid "976cc8f2-04f3-53c2-a86c-c645ed2fbec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi n}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420087} {:answer/id #uuid "da650f9b-762e-5944-b6e9-28cdf4865151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420082} :answer-field/id #uuid "02673689-46fa-584e-88ff-1a1c967a1b0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b8302ddf-29cf-570b-b7dd-dab545b3b824" :question/math-academy-id "q-162647" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+{e}^{t-1}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\nt+\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component ${e}^{t-1}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component ${t}^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."} :step/id #uuid "0dbe3293-2708-527b-b605-9fe12b6e37f9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1000e325-da05-5f3a-9e1d-24312518777d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in (-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420080} {:answer/id #uuid "2478ad2d-b6eb-59d1-b76b-abe7a69ec247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420081} {:answer/id #uuid "691ebfd7-4000-5575-996f-d102ad66730b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in (-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420084} {:answer/id #uuid "94b764b3-edc6-585d-9049-19b093287871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in (-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420086} {:answer/id #uuid "b312f764-3451-5f4e-9cfb-871263b18b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\in (0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420088}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420080} :answer-field/id #uuid "02381afc-745d-58f1-8f0d-9b55ea92ca31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7a104075-f533-58ab-b79b-2a5e66b667e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162660" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=⟨{e}^{-t},\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component ${e}^{-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\frac{1}{t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ is $t\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a5/a55ef2c927b26c9beba9b44f669c817cf749b79de163b4bf0505d15c6ec31630.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$"} :step/id #uuid "69b7e384-7af7-55ae-bcb8-73eecb04e14a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "590ce956-fa57-52c8-b676-c7a081a32b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276280} {:answer/id #uuid "8d377bcc-370f-51f9-b994-f8649f51617b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (-\\infty ,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276755} {:answer/id #uuid "928012b6-5d37-57e3-ab1c-585b2c67c370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276802} {:answer/id #uuid "c07039d3-aec7-5e92-9950-c14bc1351f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277186} {:answer/id #uuid "eee05247-e752-5968-abb0-796894121233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276280} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c4c6d8a-57de-5558-ae04-0e3791190f90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "56023ec9-5ce1-5f4b-8f47-3c4d6d6b2dd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-160789" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+s),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-s),\\,\\frac{1}{1+s}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+s)$ is $s\\in (-1,\\infty )$ .\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-s)$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- The domain of $\\frac{1}{1+s}$ is  $s\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3a/3ac2933afc460e42ad5bffb8b01b9fc3ff9275ab3bcf2ac3ab710a8094b36637.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is\n\n$\\,$ $s\\in (-1,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "743b0666-1b94-5795-b0db-227efb4df31d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420097}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "138b2176-ecc6-57f2-bc6d-f1652a33636a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278767} {:answer/id #uuid "2444e896-56b2-5fe6-9c12-ded17b4df5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278916} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb99ab6-2b20-5f51-ac07-2107a5729fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279469} {:answer/id #uuid "9484619f-2ead-592d-844c-a164a8fc5f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279912} {:answer/id #uuid "d1436dab-1fe3-50c6-a9d0-651b17cd161f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi n}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280437}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278767} :answer-field/id #uuid "064586b1-f0a2-55e9-ab4f-1edb28541b5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "355588e1-74ab-5c1c-9491-56bf772d1b60" :question/math-academy-id "q-162645" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j+\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component ${t}^{3}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\nt+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."} :step/id #uuid "85c58b22-fe0d-57c2-b692-2346cf29a3ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172da4c2-2750-5a8d-a86a-b97c9604fbad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278799} {:answer/id #uuid "7684847d-5d27-5cac-b143-1ec0db86e152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279647} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea5e2b2-027e-5a73-b7b3-004b4999d8bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in [0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279999} {:answer/id #uuid "9fec719b-cb40-5f1c-9f1f-fe5c3833e5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (-\\infty ,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280014} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ac898a-8049-5e2b-8b24-531f56c455b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in [0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280697}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280697} :answer-field/id #uuid "951bda09-c9a0-55cd-9b45-dcd0ac3017cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "86e56d87-8f7f-5aac-9c51-653244b0266e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162659" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)=⟨\\sqrt{s},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-s),\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\sqrt{s}$ is $s\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-s)$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,2).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{3}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/80/808abeaa59d7bcea3b24e741fde5296791de32b4170d60f05f2a5af203c20ba2.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is $s\\in [0,2).$"} :step/id #uuid "e7ce2d54-5c14-540f-93a3-4a8f924d9288" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420095}}] :activity/title "The Domain of a Vector-Valued Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420079}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116060} :activity/id #uuid "e6144229-d720-5bc8-9e1a-98e1705bfbbe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049527 :topic/id #uuid "fc4d066d-867b-5a4a-be7f-2ddaa87bbc33" :topic/math-academy-id 1583} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116478 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the equation $3^{3x} = 81^{x-1},$ which has two exponential terms. How do we solve it?\n\nTo solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using the fact that $81 = {\\color{red}3}^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3}^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^4\\right)^{x-1}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3x} & ={3}^{4(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =4(x-1) \\\\ 3x & =4x-4 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhenever we can rewrite an equation so that both sides have a common base, we can solve the equation by equating the exponents." :tutorial/id #uuid "91b0d933-a432-59bd-b187-7dd052926b12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2798 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "19d6b9e6-6845-5508-9a53-13a6d18de9d4" :step/math-academy-id 14505 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115029 :question/id #uuid "baae925b-d23d-56db-b15f-b438f68df608" :question/math-academy-id "e-4101"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050697}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335910}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting One Exponent"} :step/id #uuid "43796675-9206-5f0a-a634-378038c51c7d" :step/math-academy-id 14516 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115048 :question/id #uuid "e73675af-973f-583e-9909-0eb9630fdcc8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4161"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410434}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "bd0d0141-6460-5be8-8511-503ece684172" :step/math-academy-id 15049}] :activity/title "Solving Exponential Equations With Different Bases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115417}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163068} :activity/id #uuid "fdff7ccc-5a47-5748-b28e-bcf8de0682a8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049674 :topic/id #uuid "09e82368-7656-5709-b72f-b4db605d6406" :topic/math-academy-id 3013} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163326 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the sample mean, denoted $\\overline{X},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is a random variable whose probability distribution (also called the **sampling distribution**) has an expected value of $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nLet's now find the variance of the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ Using the properties of variance for sums of independent random variables, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot ({\\sigma }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+⋯+{\\sigma }^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot n{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is, it has mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58763c8a-23b3-55bb-9b5c-4781a3c271b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6276 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0fb9bf51-5dff-5207-8c2c-54d18ba6afd9" :step/math-academy-id 38034 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162106 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161672 :question/id #uuid "486f4292-2f50-5d89-be1a-b3c054b449e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10082"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebcdc0f-dc9b-54ec-9cc5-49b2c046e8b7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050606}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418457}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance of a Sample Mean"} :step/id #uuid "0828c240-f63f-5c50-a2e8-eba11979ee4c" :step/math-academy-id 38036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161673 :question/id #uuid "41713dda-2195-5bd5-b0be-941b8188cb25" :question/math-academy-id "e-10083"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418459}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"} :step/id #uuid "ae3ab16a-d830-544c-8331-c62d1505d7dd" :step/math-academy-id 38037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161674 :question/id #uuid "0f59a118-79ce-5746-8917-6f4092dcbc6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10084"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050383}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418455}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"} :step/id #uuid "6c760552-8a9d-54b8-962b-87e6f849c3da" :step/math-academy-id 38040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163333 :tutorial/content "The standard deviation of the sample mean is called the **standard error** of the sample mean and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}]\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]}\n$$\n\nSo, if we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the  standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c2217692-f38f-597c-9295-cd25f1d4d026" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6300 :tutorial/title "The Standard Error"} :step/id #uuid "2f745e7f-72c3-508a-96ad-ef71ea659d96" :step/math-academy-id 38149 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163334 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample ![](images/ff/ff40ddf134336ef15bcaf81b5b23aa8fb7c5ea13276e0f86fa6dde15841e0c5d.svg) of size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) from a population with mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) and variance ![](images/1a/1af459c3f0307649888db8b148eef968a9c5da6a77788c4ab7fe32d6ea30717e.svg) We know that the sample mean \n\n![](images/51/513b88bd8fa93336cc949758da79cac238f871b477d1682ab6b20635a4149361.svg)\n\nis an unbiased estimator of ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) \n\nHowever, it should be noted that selecting a single observation ![](images/3c/3c8b064868e0622effd6b83427f453ecdb3e033319c9e02969bb988b22ec7cb6.svg) to represent the whole could *also* be considered an unbiased estimator of the population mean. Recall that, by assumption\n\n![](images/e7/e7cc0631f5e6b060f0b9fc77dcac428c2b853d0e19be2e9b20e6e9f8d5d7aaad.svg)\n\nIn what sense is ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) a \"better\" estimator of ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) than, say, ![](images/c3/c3bb2b6f3769acd8b9c9ce9f929b5bc6c7ec840ab18503cf4c878a1fe8f8f41d.svg) The answer lies in their respective variances. \n\nAs the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) becomes larger and larger, the variance and standard error of ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) become smaller and smaller. It is easy to see that, as ![](images/f4/f4d85c2aac179db9322aef2478cfa080e9d602f8aeb8c0436f646f0e2321c139.svg)\n we have\n![](images/d5/d5676c70b4fcb7f68704ba0bb46fc262c7d8f764748e252aa989d6e82e88283b.svg)\n\nSo, when the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is large, there is less variance in the sampling distribution of ![](images/dc/dc01b2699b68bb8be585039efc249eaeeba5e4ad17a5c687a8811bf266cabc87.svg) Therefore, the larger the sample, the more likely it is that ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) will give good estimates for ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) The individual sample elements do not have this property since ![](images/63/631e2156cfdd9541694e1238fb8c717e721e0ef452de4bb898beb2c4509abc6b.svg)\n\nThe key takeaway is that the sample mean ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) becomes a better estimator for the corresponding population mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) as the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) increases." :tutorial/id #uuid "2fc54f89-7780-558c-8193-28d92a025bc8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6301 :tutorial/title "The Sample Mean and Large Samples"} :step/id #uuid "c79d4341-23ea-55d9-84aa-e073291790d1" :step/math-academy-id 38150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163071}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sample Means" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162169}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106620} :activity/id #uuid "f530f18b-11b2-5bde-8332-6af2fe3c20eb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050356 :topic/id #uuid "421dabda-4549-5ad6-b909-e9e0b37e1c41" :topic/math-academy-id 1286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107074 :tutorial/content "The **scalar triple product** of three vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ is defined as $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$\n\nFor example, suppose we have three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle 1,1,-2 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle 0,3,1 \\rangle.$ Let's compute their scalar triple product, $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$\n\nFirst, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =7i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨7,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 7+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The scalar triple product takes its name from the fact that it multiplies three vectors together and always results in a scalar.\n\nLater, we will learn some geometric intuition behind the scalar triple product. But for now, we will focus on computing the scalar triple product." :tutorial/id #uuid "28eff4c1-2c88-5799-b15d-0ab9f27be09b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2952 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "54962f85-eb4f-5689-8e98-692b298d15d1" :step/math-academy-id 15857 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106621}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105593 :question/id #uuid "2f380683-0d93-541d-a07d-95a1d889c761" :question/math-academy-id "e-4347"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ba6b6b5-ac1d-5a3a-876a-af51fdf50e0a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341978}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using the Definition"} :step/id #uuid "3a87ffcc-ec7d-55bd-9fb9-bf8d091686aa" :step/math-academy-id 15858 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107075 :tutorial/content "It turns out that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nThat is to say, if $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA proof of this result will be given at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's focus on computing the scalar triple product using the determinant above." :tutorial/id #uuid "67915540-ba40-5a4a-a7b0-6d81497d64c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2953 :tutorial/title "The Definition of the Scalar Triple Product Via the Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "49837287-74a9-55df-aeef-eb0b8fd6f361" :step/math-academy-id 15859 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105594 :question/id #uuid "b5cf98f2-087d-580c-a2f6-c37ad2af4c2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4348"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419787}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "8fd2fcb8-9be2-5a30-a126-347cf62d3bd6" :step/math-academy-id 15860 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107076 :tutorial/content "The scalar triple product has two important properties.\n\nFirst, scalars can be factored out of the product. For example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{a}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot ({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{c}) = {\\color{red}2}\\left[ \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\right].\n$$\n\nSecond, if we swap exactly two vectors in the scalar triple product, the result changes sign. For example, swapping $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\cdot ( {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = - {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\cdot ( {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n$$\n\nNote that if we swap twice, then the result remains the same, because the negatives cancel out. For example, swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}$ and then swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-b\\cdot (a\\times c) \\\\  & =-(-b\\cdot (c\\times a)) \\\\  & =b\\cdot (c\\times a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese properties arise naturally from the fact that the scalar triple product can be computed as a determinant.\n\n- If we multiply a row of the matrix by a number, then the determinant is multiplied by that number:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1} & 2{a}_{2} & 2{a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\n- If we swap two rows, then the determinant changes sign:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41cd1550-dd91-5029-a397-fa1417d1e160" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2954 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Scalar Triple Product"} :step/id #uuid "29a88567-7fa3-590d-9466-ca0095d38194" :step/math-academy-id 15861 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106625}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105595 :question/id #uuid "483b758a-9b14-5a72-87e5-70f27a0acf48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4349"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0b4d33-7699-5068-90f1-663534cd662d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419786}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Scalar Triple Products Using Its Properties"} :step/id #uuid "06a0d838-fa82-5fdb-bf4d-ea928e28bcaa" :step/math-academy-id 15862 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107077 :tutorial/content "We have been using the fact for three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ the scalar triple product can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut why is this true? To understand where this result comes from, first notice that in the formula for the triple product, the cross product can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a},$ we get an expression that is just the cofactor expansion across the first row of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =({a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k)\\cdot (|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k) \\\\  & ={a}_{1}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|-{a}_{2}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|+{a}_{3}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "99f752d5-13c8-5b3e-aeee-383770b0db33" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2955 :tutorial/title "A Proof of the Formula for the Scalar Triple Product"} :step/id #uuid "4b903237-363d-5bc1-826b-fb982e1c9d41" :step/math-academy-id 15863}] :activity/title "The Scalar Triple Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105963}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408114} :activity/id #uuid "541e0bf9-d38c-5752-a23f-c4c9f0e075e8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050356 :topic/id #uuid "421dabda-4549-5ad6-b909-e9e0b37e1c41" :topic/math-academy-id 1286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9f5fa0-b5b6-5984-816e-05ebf16c604e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261385} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e506eb-0c73-54cd-bea5-69edf125c21e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261741} {:answer/id #uuid "daff916a-8d3a-5192-a81e-8d54ac5e6b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261811} {:answer/id #uuid "f263a5a4-8c23-5633-8f3f-d1dee00dda4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261994} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a8659d-0b1d-58eb-a190-a4dc181002b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262004} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d44782d-0883-5e02-9033-b08b78209c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6324ef53-4779-5e72-aa0d-0e3d70a28579" :question/math-academy-id "q-128356" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (b\\times a),$ if $b\\times c=⟨6,-1,3⟩$ and $a=⟨3,5,-4⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨3,5,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6+5\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18-5-12 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "09e1afca-7cd5-5ac9-8a1c-381614403a24" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408117}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ef0874-008a-597f-b2cb-02be44cd3944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408103} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8dcecd-d942-5c7a-b18e-cb5a2602acc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408104} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf98593-9506-526d-9be6-5176a031c0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408105} {:answer/id #uuid "a019b149-6685-5c94-9ead-07affcb89def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408106} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d39875-cd66-5fd6-9485-32650cce5157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408103} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd432bd-eb14-5f9d-bc51-e98dfa9d5d17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32b7570a-855d-5799-88e4-c24fe54119de" :question/math-academy-id "q-20053" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-2,2,-3⟩$, $b=⟨2,-1,2⟩$ and $c=⟨-2,1,1⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)-2(2\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2)-3(2\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =6-12+0 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9d367acc-118a-5937-a41c-6d7159bd7657" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408115}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8bb8d9-a3bb-5f41-8ede-1ac64d1b2be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285204} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6f5fc5-825c-54cb-8f8c-ee058c85459b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286495} {:answer/id #uuid "eef31947-0a99-57ff-8618-686a5c2e30a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286980} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a78fa7-b3e6-5170-9dc3-909924cfd396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287018} {:answer/id #uuid "f992ea26-69d2-5514-84ea-1987e40856cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286495} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffd48eaf-9d9e-5aaf-ad75-c7d452ae0613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0db80f94-e2a5-583e-875d-ce7dd7157cf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-20062" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (b\\times a)$, if $b\\times c=⟨2,3,0⟩$ and $a=⟨1,-2,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2-6+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bf136a46-14cf-58e5-ae21-1b4fd4c26349" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408116}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a8d4cf-2a52-5eb3-932c-de54f969d498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339176} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2029a1-5daa-5d17-915b-5cb3db9d0a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339665} {:answer/id #uuid "7364ef2c-a924-50d5-ae40-c9a6106f8bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340152} {:answer/id #uuid "84d38775-5cfe-5497-a2ea-945dc01057b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340321} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f005ac-dfb9-5a8e-9f29-140e8e17a482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340321} :answer-field/id #uuid "50438105-46e0-54c5-8329-966f684eb5e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fc420fe9-802f-5344-9de7-9b53d8435b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-73462" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨3,1,2⟩$, $q=⟨1,-2,1⟩$ and $r=⟨4,1,-2⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $q,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (q\\times p) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)-(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)-2(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-20+1-14 \\\\  & =-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c9595afb-61e2-5d8f-929b-2f523416e52a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408113}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a28f1b85-eac0-597c-848e-a2bdbc20cee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408107} :answer-field/id #uuid "9284f3f7-2649-559e-ade1-b845ad584b38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "627a3422-ad6a-5d23-b76b-6cf6cb390db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-317318" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-1,2,4⟩$, $b=⟨0,3,1⟩$ and $c=⟨2,5,0⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n\n$-15$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\,-\\,2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\,+\\,4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\,(3\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 5)\\,-\\,2\\,(0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)\\,+\\,4\\,(0\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\,(-5)\\,-\\,2\\,(-2)\\,+\\,4\\,(-6) \\\\  & =5+4-24 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ca58aca0-69d3-55b1-a46c-485edbd29743"}] :activity/title "The Scalar Triple Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408102}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419792} :activity/id #uuid "e3670cc4-3e0e-52dd-b203-84b0f2da8c90" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050356 :topic/id #uuid "421dabda-4549-5ad6-b909-e9e0b37e1c41" :topic/math-academy-id 1286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419788 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a8d4cf-2a52-5eb3-932c-de54f969d498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339176} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2029a1-5daa-5d17-915b-5cb3db9d0a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339665} {:answer/id #uuid "7364ef2c-a924-50d5-ae40-c9a6106f8bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340152} {:answer/id #uuid "84d38775-5cfe-5497-a2ea-945dc01057b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340321} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f005ac-dfb9-5a8e-9f29-140e8e17a482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340321} :answer-field/id #uuid "50438105-46e0-54c5-8329-966f684eb5e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fc420fe9-802f-5344-9de7-9b53d8435b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-73462" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨3,1,2⟩$, $q=⟨1,-2,1⟩$ and $r=⟨4,1,-2⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $q,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (q\\times p) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)-(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)-2(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-20+1-14 \\\\  & =-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2145564f-b143-5694-829e-a9e30f5dc092" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419790}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419789 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069decae-ea7d-53a6-9d7f-6cd1604d5e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419779} :answer-field/id #uuid "0082553f-6022-5a48-bc6b-791b1c483f07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "944d3b7e-a470-59f8-92c4-a8abf2a8b72b" :question/math-academy-id "q-317329" :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot (c\\times a)$, if $-5a\\cdot ((6b)\\times (4c))=120.$\n\n$b\\cdot (c\\times a)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5a\\cdot ((6b)\\times (4c)) & =120 \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 6\\cdot 4\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =120 \\\\ (-120)[a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =120 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (c\\times a) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "69f520c8-2f68-5cc8-8bb4-1d0fcdb03028"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aadbe221-860c-5e54-a0de-bd7fb0bef36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419781}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419781} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea71914-4f17-5f64-8016-140e481b698d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "20209d26-6af3-5176-8409-17df2aefc0b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-317313" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨1,-3,0⟩$, $b=⟨-2,2,0⟩$ and $c=⟨2,0,-4⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}|\\,-\\,(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}|\\,+\\,0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\,(2\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot 0)\\,-\\,(-3)\\,((-2)\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot 2)\\,+\\,0\\,((-2)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1\\,(-8)\\,-\\,(-3)\\,(8)\\,+\\,0\\,(-4) \\\\  & =-8+24+0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7ce27cb2-7851-562f-a805-9613dfe990e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419791}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f56b9f8-00ca-5814-abb9-6c5a7caba35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419780} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd14090-bb12-5afc-8250-d9460b7c0f27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f15d30b5-fe4a-5207-9935-fd7503715554" :question/math-academy-id "q-317326" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-2,0,-1⟩$, $b=⟨-3,-1,4⟩$ and $c=⟨5,-2,-2⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\,-\\,0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\,+\\,(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\,((-1)(-2)-4\\cdot (-2))\\,-\\,0\\,((-3)\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot 5)\\,+\\,(-1)\\,((-3)\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =(-2)\\,(10)\\,-\\,0\\,(-14)\\,+\\,(-1)\\,(11) \\\\  & =-20+0-11 \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "85680ae8-e720-54c1-a502-614781ce6410" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419789}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "343abe13-b014-58c0-913d-2fc9f7a11d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285270} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3b1e45-a9c8-5b28-b558-ec4bc793eb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285496} {:answer/id #uuid "a878dcad-4ddf-585b-a29e-6f1bfa1aa273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286324} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e6bcc3-f1d7-59c6-953b-c03961aeaf47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286630} {:answer/id #uuid "fbedc400-7438-5567-8dfa-d1cfcb39be79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287087} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a770db-2d9f-5df9-858e-0db4cb77fe94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae812812-92a4-5aa7-85f9-50adcd0043cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-20059" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (a\\times b)$, if $3a\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}b\\times 3c)=18\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}b\\times 3c) & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $c$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (c\\times b) & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $c$ and $a$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d94231c6-3192-5dc5-bd63-54e41758bbbe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419788}}] :activity/title "The Scalar Triple Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419778}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106573} :activity/id #uuid "0a27c7be-9281-5ee1-a624-d9ff46333578" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050438 :topic/id #uuid "493479ab-e6ae-52f5-9c1a-827aa0443283" :topic/math-academy-id 1359} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107065 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ below. How can we determine the intervals where $f'(x)$ is positive or negative?\n\nThe first step is to identify the points where the derivative is zero. Here, the critical points are $x=2$ (a local maximum) and $x=4$ (a local minimum), and $f'(x) = 0$ at these points.\n\n![](images/de/de01a8dec747f86d6b276431ef02675d3827028508be9aa9ca3699ca4e6bcd6c.png)\n\nThe next step is to identify where the function is increasing and decreasing. Below, the intervals where $f(x)$ is increasing are shown in blue, and the intervals where $f(x)$ is decreasing are shown in green.\n\n![](images/10/10edc10cefa3cc48713795e0e2f40b05ba6174e2da7aa5698a02fc35444b1c45.png)\n\nSince we know where the function is increasing or decreasing, we can determine where $f'(x)$ is positive or negative.\n\n- When $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, the slopes of the tangent lines are positive, so we have \n\n$$\nf'(x)>0.\n$$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the slopes of the tangent lines are negative, so we have \n\n$$\nf'(x)<0.\n$$\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be  summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,2) & 2 & (2,4) & 4 & (4,+\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{'} & + & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, \n\n- $f'(x)>0$ on $(-\\infty,2) \\cup (4,+\\infty),$ while\n\n- $f'(x)<0$ on $(2,4).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a75bc2f2-f4fa-50c8-9eaa-230212f35132" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2661 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "136ca6f2-4f48-519a-8e2b-fcd52eaff347" :step/math-academy-id 12776 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105708 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105566 :question/id #uuid "6b6ba1b8-5041-57c3-bcbb-239af0faa4dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3722"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341972}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative"} :step/id #uuid "fe83e38b-e03f-5af9-907e-72d9f01b87e5" :step/math-academy-id 12783 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107067 :tutorial/content "To determine whether a function $f(x)$ is increasing or decreasing, we can calculate the derivative and then solve the appropriate inequality.\n\n- To find where $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we solve the inequality $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- To find where $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, we solve the inequality $f'(x) < 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9bdb8755-6e15-5d0b-b4da-b1a720b6dc4e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2665 :tutorial/title "Calculating Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing or Decreasing Using Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "bff9aeb3-19b2-547d-b422-3a3680105413" :step/math-academy-id 12785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105568 :question/id #uuid "7be416a1-e729-58a4-bc3c-d622d058eba8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3728"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050617}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400667}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "10ad6cd4-75ee-5a9e-9c37-f75681712cd6" :step/math-academy-id 12790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105571 :question/id #uuid "cc1f0cb5-5cca-5a70-a89f-2983a64e273f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3731"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402991}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "371b12ac-a46c-5fdd-8712-331bed644307" :step/math-academy-id 12793 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105572 :question/id #uuid "88cdcba5-2572-5a6a-aa99-826b80cb1d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-3734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341982}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points"} :step/id #uuid "27be8546-1f29-51fe-90dd-a240e31ced9f" :step/math-academy-id 12796}] :activity/title "Determining Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing or Decreasing" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105992}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162825} :activity/id #uuid "ccbd1570-797c-5564-8927-e502aa05caec" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527 :topic/id #uuid "508ac00e-0867-53e8-a192-d981ff4bbdc2" :topic/math-academy-id 3029} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163179 :tutorial/content "We can do the same trick using the **elementary column operations** instead of row operations.\n\nThere are three types of elementary *column* operations, which are entirely analogous to the corresponding row operations:\n\n- Switching any two columns: \n\n$$\nC_i \\leftrightarrow C_j\n$$\n\n- Multiplying (each element of) a column by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nC_i := k C_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- Adding a multiple of one column to another column: \n\n$$\nC_j := C_j+k C_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** \n\n- Unlike elementary row operations, elementary *column* operations change the solutions of a system of linear equations. This is because column operations alter the coefficients associated with each variable across the system, changing the equations.\n\n- The exception is swapping two columns - this doesn't alter the solutions *provided that* we swap the corresponding variables in the solution vector. For example, if we swap the first and second columns, we must treat the second variable as the first and vice versa. The solution set remains unchanged because the relationships between the variables and their coefficients are preserved." :tutorial/id #uuid "e947134c-e909-5a3b-85a6-d284e04816e0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10652 :tutorial/title "Column Operations"} :step/id #uuid "ce002306-ea12-5d28-8c26-a1710d2f4327" :step/math-academy-id 218771 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162829}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163214 :tutorial/content "When studying linear algebra, it is common to use elementary row operations to reduce a matrix $A$ to another matrix $B$ such that $B$ contains many zeros. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the entry $a_{11}$ located in the top left-hand corner. We can reduce *all* of the entries *below* it using elementary row operations.\n\nFirst, we subtract $3$ times the first row from the second row.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $5$ times the first row to the third row.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! We have successfully performed elementary row operations to reduce all of the entries below $a_{11}$ to zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "1e0cae18-aa8d-5b1d-9aab-4a5334dec0c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4828 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b68078fb-1628-5a76-bb97-1c1261f6641d" :step/math-academy-id 30052 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161965 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161757 :question/id #uuid "70991204-370a-5804-a04e-7e664db052de" :question/math-academy-id "e-8048"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f1ec99-e166-5986-a07d-f07be0dfeb31" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404530}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8918fe56-d9e1-5eae-97d1-dfc810e443e6" :step/math-academy-id 30053 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161966 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161758 :question/id #uuid "cbf8e85c-ffa6-55b9-a087-2a5272f10f25" :question/math-academy-i